==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|stěd}}, {{IPA|en|/stɛd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-stead.ogg|a=US}}
*: {{rhymes|en|ɛd|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*steh₂-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|sted|sted, stede|pos=noun}} and {{m|enm|steden|pos=verb}}, from {{inh|en|ang|stede}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*stadiz||[[place]]}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*stéh₂tis||[[standing]], [[location]]}}. {{doublet|en|stad}}.

{{col-top|2|cognates and related terms}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|steid||location, place}}, {{cog|frr|Stair}}, {{m|frr|Stat}}, {{m|frr|steed}}, {{m|frr|stää||city, town; place, stead}}, {{cog|stq|Steede||place, stead}}, {{m|stq|Stääd||city, town}}, {{cog|fy|stêd||city, town}}, {{cog|bar|Stådt||city, town}}, {{cog|nl|stad}}, {{m|nl|stede||city, town}}, {{cog|de|Stadt||city, town}}, {{m|de|Statt||abode, place, stead}}, {{m|de|Stätte||place, spot, venue}}, {{cog|nds-de|Stee||location, place}}, {{cog|lb|Stad||city, town}}, {{cog|wym|śtaod||city, town}}, {{cog|yi|שטאָט||city, town}}, {{cog|da|-}}, {{cog|nb|stad||city, town}}, {{m|nb|sted||place}}, {{cog|fo|stað||place}}, {{m|fo|staður||location, place; town}}, {{cog|is|staður||location, place}}, {{cog|nrn|sta||domicile, farm}}, {{cog|nn|stad||place; city, town}}, {{cog|sv|stad}}, {{m|sv|stadh}}, {{m|sv|stedt||city, town}}, {{cog|got|𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌸𐍃||location, place}}. See the doublet {{doublet|en|stasis|notext=-}}.
{{col-bottom}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|of a person or thing}} The [[position]] or [[function]] (of someone or something), as taken on by a successor. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Persuasion|text=She was so wretched and so vehement, complained so much of injustice in being expected to go away instead of Anne; Anne, who was nothing to Louisa, while she was her sister, and had the best right to stay in Henrietta's '''stead'''!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|author=Muriel Saint Clare Byrne|title=Elizabethan Life in Town and Country|page=285
|passage=His nurse had told him all about changelings, and how the little people would always try to steal a beautiful human child out of its cradle and put in its '''stead''' one of their own ailing, puking brats {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=31 March 2011|title=Kin selection
|passage=Had Daniel Ortega not got himself illegally on to this year’s ballot to seek a third term, his wife might have run in his '''stead'''.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[place]] as it relates to a [[role]], [[service]], or [[ability]]; [[capacity]].
# {{lb|en|figurative|uncountable}} A relational or circumstantial [[position]]; [[standing]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=19 September 2010|author=Dan van der Vat|journal=w:The Guardian
|passage=Though small and delicate-looking, she gave an impression of intense earnestness and latent toughness, qualities that stood her in good '''stead''' when she dared to challenge the most intrusive communist society in eastern Europe.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|countable}} A [[place]] as it relates to [[situation]], [[circumstance]], or [[status]]; [[condition]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Sonnia De Guzman|title=Collected Poems|page=41|passage=I shiver to the cold solitude <br>Overwhelming desire to have a different '''stead''' <br>I don't know why <br>I don't know why <br>I'm just afraid to fly {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Michael Moran|title=Private Foundations and Development Partnerships|passage=It is not only the sheer size of its endowment or the range and extent of its network ties that place it in a different '''stead''' to other unofficial actors.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|literary}} A [[place]], or [[spot]], in general; [[location]]. {{defdate|10th|19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|II|text=For he ne wonneth in one certaine '''stead''', / But restlesse walketh all the world around{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=James Meek|title=To Calais, In Ordinary Time|passage="{{...}} I went to another '''stead''' in the yard to be further from her cries."}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A place where a person normally rests; a [[seat]]. {{defdate|10th|18th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1633|author=P. Fletcher|title=Purple Island
|passage=There now the hart, fearlesse of greyhound, feeds, / And loving pelican in safety breeds; / There shrieking satyres fill the people's emptie '''steads'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[inhabited]] place; a [[settlement]], [[city]], [[town]] etc. {{defdate|13th|16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} An [[estate]], a [[property]] with its grounds; a [[farm]]; a [[homestead]]. 
#* {{RQ:Haggard Allan's Wife|page=123|passage=But of course I could not do this by myself, so I took a Hottentot—a very clever man when he was not drunk—who lived on the '''stead''', into my confidence.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[frame]] on which a bed is laid; a [[bedstead]]. {{defdate|15th|19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1693|author=w:John Dryden|title=Baucis and Philemon
|passage=The genial bed / Sallow the feet, the borders, and the '''stead'''.}}

